## Prior Art Directive (David, 2026-09-06 — binding on every seat)

Any algorithm, selection rule, data structure, proof technique, or
optimization you implement or propose MUST be annotated with the prior
art you know of: paper/system/author and year, and what you took from
it versus what is yours. Put it in three places: (1) a comment at the
code site, (2) the ledger entry, (3) the final report under a "Prior
art" heading. If you know of none, write "no prior art known to me"
explicitly — silence is not allowed. If you cannot verify (no network
in the sandbox), write "unverified — lead to check" and name the
search terms. Re-deriving a known idea is fine and often the right
move; presenting it without the annotation is a reporting failure.
The lead checks the literature through the proxy and adds citations;
your job is to make the check possible. Receipt for why this exists:
APA-SP1 reproduced the BLASST (arXiv 2512.12087) running-max criterion
without citing it — see /mnt/Shared/APA_SP_Prior_Art_Comparison_2026-09-06.md.

## 10. Infrastructure Repos: Fork, Test, Reclaim (David, 2026-10-02, binding)

David: "Infrastructure repos require forking the repo locally to make
changes to test. Then reclaim and integrate the changes into main
afterwards."

- **What counts as infrastructure: the metal level.** That means
  Project-Tensor, GraftRepository (GRM), and anything else that works at
  the metal level (kernels, engines, runtime memory). David: "GRAPA isn't
  infra - GRM, Project Tensor, anything that works at the metal level."
  Every other repo is a project repo.
- **Never change or test inside the production infra repo.** No experiment
  branches, no worktrees, no seat runs and no builds there. The rule is
  enforced: the guard denies new branches in infra repos except
  `reclaim/*`.
- **Fork.** All edits, builds, seats and GPU cells run in a fork: a local
  clone at `<Repo>/Fork/<project>_fork_<name>`, with the push URL disabled
  and `Fork/` in the production repo's `.git/info/exclude`. Use the helper
  `infra-fork <repo> <name>`. Until it exists, do the same by hand:
  - `git clone <repo> <repo>/Fork/<project>_fork_<name>`;
  - `git -C <fork> remote set-url --push origin DISABLED-infra-fork`.
- **Reclaim.** When the work is done and receipted, the lead fetches the
  fork's branch into production as `reclaim/<name>`:
  `git -C <repo> fetch <fork> <branch>:reclaim/<name>`, or the helper
  `reclaim`. Production pulls from the fork; a fork never pushes.
- **Integrate.** Merging `reclaim/<name>` into `main` needs a promotion
  ticket (`promote`). The ticket carries:
  - David's verbatim approval;
  - a cross-model blind review receipt (§8), or his verbatim waiver;
  - a scope statement (§2): what it covers and what it does not.

  Every infra promotion is forwarded to David.
- **Project repos** keep in-project worktrees (global CLAUDE.md), and their
  leads promote on their own judgment. The ticket needs a scope statement
  and a review: a receipt, or `lead-verified: <what was checked>`.
- **Enforcement.** `reference-transaction` + `pre-push` hooks in every repo
  (SystemOps GG1). `--no-verify`, `-c core.hooksPath` or editing the hooks
  to get past them is a violation of this rule, not a workaround.
